Wow, my first post here and it's about dorky computer stuff.
 
Unix, if you haven't noticed is the least "compatible" OS that exists (and if 
you've tried to compile nh313 on some whizbang clone UX, you'll understand
completely).  And "curses" is one of the least compatible packages in unix.
Blah.  There are two overwhelmingly seperate and distinct "flavors" of
curses (kinda like USVr3 and BSD), called termcap and terminfo which take
major tweaking to be able to cross compile between them.  yuckola.

Nethack simply reads in the codes for your particular terminal from whatever
database is available and uses them in their own functions (so does vi).
Using curses, as a library, is nice to do screen stuff, and it's great for
building text-oriented user interface libraries (which is what I mainly do),
but it's not faster than running the codes out raw yourself.
 
